& [ResearchAnchors]
  + NIST SP 800-57 Part 1 Revision 5 defines proof of possession as assurance that the owner of a key pair has the associated private key while treating storage protection cryptoperiod inventory recovery compromise and other key-management controls as additional concerns: https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/57/pt1/r5/final
  + RFC 9449 demonstrates application-level proof of possession by a context-bound signature and states that such a proof by itself is not authentication or an access-control decision: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9449.html
  + RFC 9449 also requires endpoint or purpose binding unique proof identity bounded lifetime and replay defenses; a deterministic fixture or caller label cannot establish those live properties
  + this seam therefore verifies proof-of-possession correspondence but keeps current exclusive trusted durable custody false until a later externally governed live challenge and custody-control admission

